## Confounders

One assumption of linear regression is that our model is correctly specifed.

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="66%"}
The previous scatterplot is an example of **omitted variable bias**. The relationship between bill depth and body mass *looks* negative, but we see clusters of *positive*-looking relationships.

Since we have worked with this data so much, we know what the omitted variable likely influencing this clustering is: [species!]{.fragment}

[Species as an omitted variable is currently **confounding** the relationship between bill depth and body mass.]{.fragment}

[We can account for species by including it as a predictor in our model, going from single to **multiple** regression!]{.fragment}
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}

