## Variance Inflation Factor

The presence of multicollinearity among predictors in the model increases the variance of the model estimates. We can examine this effect with Variance Inflation Factors ($VIF$s).

$$\mathrm{VIF_p} = \frac{1}{1 - \mathrm{SMC_p}}$$

where $p$ is a given model predictor.

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
Our penguin model:
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
car::vif(m3)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
                      GVIF Df GVIF^(1/(2*Df))
bill_depth_mm_z   4.328926  1        2.080607
bill_length_mm_z  4.744497  1        2.178187
species          13.996855  2        1.934228
```


:::
:::

:::
::::


Variance Inflation Factors indicate the factor by which the standard error has increased because of multicollinearity for that predictor.


## Tolerance

An alternative way of looking at this is with *tolerance*, which is just the inverse of $VIF$.

$$\mathrm{tolerance_p} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{VIF_p}}$$

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
Our penguin model:
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
1 / car::vif(m3)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
                       GVIF  Df GVIF^(1/(2*Df))
bill_depth_mm_z  0.23100420 1.0       0.4806290
bill_length_mm_z 0.21077049 1.0       0.4590975
species          0.07144462 0.5       0.5170022
```


:::
:::

:::
::::

Tolerance indicates the proportion of variance in a given predictor that is independent of other predictors.


